Apply order 0021 against mariopartyrd/partyboard 98ac07fd1e8f7ab938bbb6fe75789fad99419010 (CC0-1.0; ADR 0009 section 5).
hsfload: keep MaterialLoad's colour writes inside rgba, on MPT_PC
Applied in order by runtimes/gc_wii/mp4/CMakeLists.txt with `git apply`.

MaterialLoad writes each material's lit colour to rgba[i], but rgba has 100 entries and nothing
reads it. Four models on the disc have more materials (Manta Rings, Paratrooper Plunge, Toad's Quick
Draw and Bowser's Bigger Blast, 112 to 255). On the console the extra writes land in a local scratch
buffer of hsfload.c. In our module head follows rgba, so the write for material 107 overwrote
head.material.count with a negative value. The loop ended there, materials 107 and up kept their
file symbol indices as attr pointers, and MakeDisplayList faulted on the first face that used one
(Manta Rings, docs/titles/mp4.md section 21). The writes past rgba are now skipped. The colours for
materials 0-99 are unchanged, and no material is converted differently.

diff --git a/src/game/hsfload.c b/src/game/hsfload.c
index 9819ac6..e7b0d94 100644
--- a/src/game/hsfload.c
+++ b/src/game/hsfload.c
@@ -882,10 +882,17 @@ static void MaterialLoad(void)
             new_mat->attrNum = curr_mat->attrNum;
             // the pointers are being treated as values
             new_mat->attr = (intptr_t *)(NSymIndex+((uintptr_t)curr_mat->attr));
-            rgba[i].r = new_mat->litColor[0];
-            rgba[i].g = new_mat->litColor[1];
-            rgba[i].b = new_mat->litColor[2];
-            rgba[i].a = 255;
+#ifdef MPT_PC
+            /* rgba has 100 entries and nothing reads it. A model with more materials wrote past it:
+             * on the console into a scratch buffer, here into head, which ended this loop early. */
+            if (i < (s32)(sizeof(rgba) / sizeof(rgba[0])))
+#endif
+            {
+                rgba[i].r = new_mat->litColor[0];
+                rgba[i].g = new_mat->litColor[1];
+                rgba[i].b = new_mat->litColor[2];
+                rgba[i].a = 255;
+            }
             for(j=0; j<new_mat->attrNum; j++) {
                 new_mat->attr[j] = new_mat->attr[j];
             }
